Brushing Up on Networking Fundamentals: IPs, Subnets & CIDR

Understanding these basics is key for designing efficient and scalable networks.

🏠 IP Address – Your Device’s Home Address
 Every device on a network needs a unique identifier, just like every house has an address.
 Example: 192.168.1. 10
IPv4 = 4 octets × 8 bits = 32 bits
Binary: 11000000.10101000.00000001.00001010

🌆 Subnet – Neighborhoods in a City
 Subnetting divides a big network into smaller “neighborhoods” to organize traffic and improve efficiency.
 Think of it like streets in a city – mail gets delivered efficiently because each street has a range of house numbers.

🛣️ CIDR – Flexible Street Planning
 CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) defines how big each neighborhood is.
 Written as: 192.168.1. 0/24 → /24 = network bits
IPs calculation: 2^(32 - subnet bits)
CIDRIPs AvailableAnalogy/321 IPSingle house/24256 IPsSmall neighborhood/221024 IPsLarge neighborhood

✅ Key Takeaway:
 Subnetting and CIDR are like planning a city efficiently. With them, networks stay organized, scalable, and easy to manage.
